    "title": "ARKHE: A Verifiable Trust Architecture for Autonomous AI Agents",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.22760896",
    "publication_date": "2026-09-15",
    "description": "<p>Autonomous AI agents are beginning to execute consequential actions &mdash; payments, deployments,<br>signatures &mdash; on the basis of representations their principals cannot independently verify. This<br>monograph develops and examines a design answer: Arkhe, a verifiable trust layer whose load-bearing<br>elements are a fail-closed kernel of containment, cryptographic model attestation (canonicalized on the<br>GGUF open-weight container), an append-only causal evidence graph (WormGraph) anchored to<br>external transparency logs, and quorum-governed rule corpora (constitution-as-code) whose authority<br>is structurally separated from event witnesses. The study proceeds by design-science analysis with<br>systematic documentary verification. Incident reconstructions of the Bybit (2025, ~$1.5B), StablR<br>(2026, $13.5M), and SUPERFORTUNE (2026, ~$15.18M) failures are mapped to architectural<br>controls; the architecture is aligned with the EU AI Act, GDPR, Brazil's LGPD, MiCA, and ISO/IEC<br>42001; and a minimal viable product &mdash; GGUF model verification for machine-learning engineers &mdash; is<br>specified with falsifiable adoption metrics. Extending the architecture to its limiting case, the book<br>specifies a five-front evidence program for the Hodge conjecture, a Clay Millennium Prize problem,<br>integrating verified 2025-2026 results (including Markman's proof for abelian fourfolds) with Lean 4<br>formalization and Rekor anchoring, and recording three displayed corrections to the program's internal<br>claims. Counterarguments, limitations, and open problems are treated as load-bearing sections. The<br>conclusion assesses the project as architecturally coherent but pre-product, and prescribes a<br>metrics-gated path from verification artifact to market.</p>",
